Superficial thermal burn from splattering cooking oil to dorsal hand
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Scattered punctate circular erythema, Superficial fluid-filled vesicles, Splattering hot fluid mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-041
Reason for Visit: Acute, severely painful burn injuries to the hand following a hot oil exposure, requiring urgent assessment of burn depth and barrier dressing application.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted multiple hot oil burns to the dorsal hand causing intense, uncontrolled pain. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out deep partial-thickness burns involving the extensor tendons, definitively manage the severe neuropathic pain, and apply strict aseptic dressings to prevent complex hand infections before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Hot cooking oil splattered all over the back of my hand, and the stinging is agonizing when the air hits it.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of frying food when hot oil popped out of a pan 45 minutes ago. This resulted in multiple distinct punctate and small circular patches of erythema scattered over the dorsal hand. The patient experiences immediate, intense stinging pain directly corresponding to each individual splash site. There are intact, clear fluid-filled vesicles and raw weeping pink dermis where the thin skin has ruptured. There is significant pain exacerbation upon exposure to ambient air currents or minor tactile friction.
Functional Status: Fully independent; actively holding the hand elevated to avoid touching any surfaces.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor kitchen hazard; advised to use splatter guards when frying.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, in acute distress, continuously blowing on the hand to cool it or shielding it from drafts.
Physical Exam: Hand examination reveals scattered punctate circular erythema and superficial fluid-filled vesicles on the dorsal aspect. There is brisk capillary return noted within the pink wound beds. The patient has a preserved ability to actively flex and extend the fingers without restricting tendon movement, though movement is guarded due to pain. Normal sensory and vascular status distal to the injured areas is confirmed. There is a total lack of deep tissue destruction, coagulated white central lesions, or leathery eschar.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen area cleaned; stove turned off.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Applied cool water for 10 minutes prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial thermal burn to dorsal hand - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep partial-thickness/Full-thickness burn - Ruled out due to: Brisk capillary refill in the wound beds, extreme sensitivity to light touch, and lack of pale/insensate tissue.

Extensor tendon thermal injury - Ruled out due to: Intact active extension of the digits against resistance without localized tendon tethering.

Chemical burn - Ruled out due to: Clear thermal mechanism (hot cooking oil) with no exposure to caustic alkaline or acidic household agents.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Hot oil splatter burns to the dorsal hand carry a high risk for full-thickness punctate burns due to the high specific heat of oil. Bedside assessment carefully examined each lesion, confirming purely superficial partial-thickness involvement with intact deep dermal vascularity. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the underlying extensor tendons from damage and instantly deploy specialized occlusive barrier dressings, immediately terminating the severe air-exposure pain cycle and safely avoiding an unnecessary, painful transfer to an emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Gently cleansed the area with sterile saline. Intact blisters were explicitly preserved. Applied a specialized non-adherent silicone foam or hydrogel sheet to the entire dorsal hand, secured loosely with a tubular bandage to avoid restricting venous return. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the hand elevated above the level of your heart on a pillow for 48 hours. By utilizing gravity to drain the swelling away from the tight compartments of the hand, you directly stop the throbbing pain and prevent the fingers from becoming permanently stiff.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Silicone foam burn dressing.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for dressing change and inspection of blister integrity.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the fingers suddenly become pale, cold, or numb, if massive swelling prevents you from bending the fingers at all, or if thick yellow pus appears.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Burns on the back of the hand can cause significant swelling over the first 24 hours; this is a normal inflammatory response.
Clinical Course: The sharp stinging will subside within a few hours under the dressing. The blisters will slowly reabsorb or peel over the next 10-14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you notice red streaks tracking up your forearm, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The dressing acts as a physical shield, blocking the air from hitting the exposed nerves and stopping the pain. Elevating the hand prevents fluid from pooling and causing a severe throbbing ache.
Actionable Steps: Do not pop the blisters; they are nature's sterile bandage. Keep the dressing completely dry. Take ibuprofen and paracetamol together for the first 2 days.

